@NODE MainWnd "PhoenixUserCfg documentation - The main window"
@{b}The listview:@{ub}
To the left in the main window there is a list view, which contains
all users belonging to the group selected (All, unvalidated, etc). If
you click on a user's name (s)he will be loaded. The user's Phoenix
configration will be displayed in the other gadgets.

If a user didn't have a Phoenix configuration, all gadgets will be zero,
not checked, etc.

@{b}The string gadget:@{ub}
Below the listview there's a string gadget, which allways displays the
name of the user you're editing. You can also enter a name here, and the
program will try to load the user.

@{b}Unloading a user:@{ub}
If a user is about to be unloaded (A new user is being loaded, program
is about to quit etc), and any information is changed, you will be
prompted about whetever you want to save thee changes or not.

@{b}The "Max Packet" gadget:@{ub}
The user's max messages per mail packet seting. If it's higher than the
value configured in PhoenixCfg it will be ignored by the export modules.

@{b}The "Area" gadget:@{ub}
The user's max messages per area seting. If it's higher than the value
configured in PhoenixCfg it will be ignored by the export modules.

@{b}The "Wrap Msgs" gadget:@{ub}
The export modules will try to wrap messages at this x-position. Zero
means 78.

@{b}The "Pkt Type" gadget:@{ub}
Which mail format the user wants the mail packets in. Beware that
Phoenix doesn't auto-detect which mail format a reply packet is in.

@{b}The "Archiver" gadget:@{ub}
Which archiver mail packets should be archived with. The archiver's are
configured in Zeus' archivers program. Phoenix auto-detects which
archiver is used to archive a reply packet.

@{b}The "Flags" gadget:@{ub}
Opens the @{"flags window" Link FlagsWnd}.

@{b}The "Save" gadget:@{ub}
Saves the changes made to disc.

@{b}The "Quit" gadget:@{ub}
Quits the program.
@ENDNODE

@NODE FlagsWnd "PhoenixUserCfg documentation - The flags window"
@{b}The "News" gadget:@{ub}
Include the newsletter, if available, in mail packets.

@{b}The "Hello" gadget:@{ub}
Include the welcome screen, if available, in mail packets.

@{b}The "Goodbye" gadget:@{ub}
Include the goodbye screen, if available, in mail packets.

@{b}The "File list" gadget:@{ub}
Include the new files list, if available, in mail packets.

@{b}The "Index" gadget:@{ub}
Include QWK index (.NDX) files in mail packets.

@{b}The "Grab Own" gadget:@{ub}
Include messages written by the user in mail packets.

@{b}The "All New Mode" gadget:@{ub}
If the user got more unread messages than the @{"max msgs per area" Link MainWnd}
limit, Phoenix should start to dump messages from the last read pointer.
Otherwise it would start from the last message minus the "max msgs
per area" limit.

@{b}The "Bulletins" gadget:@{ub}
Include bulletins created since the user last logged on in mail
packets.

@{b}The "No Scheduler" gadget:@{ub}
The user shouldn't be able to use the scheduler system.

@{b}The "Asynch grab" gadget:@{ub}
Mail packets should be generated in the background. I.e. the user
should be returned to the BBS at once when grabbing.
@ENDNODE

@NODE Menus "PhoenixUserCfg documentation - The menu"
All the options in the menu have short-cuts. If you hold down the
left Amiga key and hits the character written to the far right of an
option, it will have the same result as choosing the option.

@{b}The "About" option:@{ub}
Prints version, copyright and general information about the
program.

@{b}The "Iconify" option:@{ub}
Closes all windows and opens an icon on Controler's screen. To
re-open the windows click on the icon.

@{b}The "Reload" option:@{ub}
Reloads the current user's Phoenix configuration.

@{b}The "Save" option:@{ub}
Saves the changes made to disc.

@{b}The "Save&Quit" option:@{ub}
Saves the changes made to disc and quits the program.

@{b}The "Quit" option:@{ub}
Quits the program.
@ENDNODE
